Closed-set accuracy and transfer to unseen classes answer different
questions. The printed verification/retrieval results and actual error images
make that distinction visible;good seen-class classification does not establish
strong unseen-class retrieval. No real-face accuracy or pretrained-model claim
follows from these digit experiments.

> Native confusion matrices

These matrices use the held-out predictions from the executed program above.
The native `ConfusionMatrix` SVG API labels exact counts with true classes
running from top to bottom and predicted classes from left to right.

\image(figures/angular_margin_confusion_api_0.svg)
\caption(Held-out classification confusion counts, evaluation 1. Exact cell counts are printed in the image.)
\alttext(Integer prediction counts with true classes as rows and predicted classes as columns.)

\image(figures/angular_margin_confusion_api_1.svg)
\caption(Held-out classification confusion counts, evaluation 2. Exact cell counts are printed in the image.)
\alttext(Integer prediction counts with true classes as rows and predicted classes as columns.)


> Conclusion

The margin geometry,normalization derivatives and label-free inference policy are explicit. Complete compact fitting and restored held-out images distinguish seen-class classification from unseen transfer. Tensor calculations preserve the source margin/tail conventions while avoiding hidden numerical evaluation loops.

> References

- [Deng et al.(2019),ArcFace:Additive Angular Margin Loss for Deep Face Recognition](https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.07698v3).
- [Wang et al.(2018),CosFace:Large Margin Cosine Loss for Deep Face Recognition](https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.09414).
